GCC's aarch64-w64-mingw32 target does not support constructor priorities (its
PE/COFF init machinery never grew the sorted-section support x86 mingw has), so
winpthreads' __attribute__((constructor(0))) on winpthreads_init() -- added
upstream so the init runs before any user constructor that might call pthread
functions -- is a hard "constructor priorities are not supported" error there,
breaking every aarch64 GCC build of winpthreads. clang supports the priority on
aarch64 PE and is unaffected.

Fall back to a plain, unprioritized __attribute__((constructor)) for GCC on
aarch64 only: that is winpthreads' pre-priority historical behavior (and what
the MSVC build's .CRT$XCT placement approximates), so the init still runs
during CRT startup, merely unordered relative to other constructors -- and the
function pointers it fills are NULL-guarded at their call sites regardless.
Self-guarding (#if defined(__aarch64__) && !defined(__clang__)), so x86 and all
clang builds are unaffected; applied unconditionally.

diff --git a/mingw-w64-libraries/winpthreads/src/misc.c b/mingw-w64-libraries/winpthreads/src/misc.c
index b2d5ceb..e2538d2 100644
--- a/mingw-w64-libraries/winpthreads/src/misc.c
+++ b/mingw-w64-libraries/winpthreads/src/misc.c
@@ -42,8 +42,16 @@ BOOL (WINAPI *_pthread_get_handle_information) (HANDLE, LPDWORD) = NULL;
 #pragma GCC diagnostic push
 #pragma GCC diagnostic ignored "-Wprio-ctor-dtor"
 #endif
+#if defined(__aarch64__) && !defined(__clang__)
+/* GCC's aarch64 PE target does not support constructor priorities and
+ * rejects them with a hard error.  Fall back to an unprioritized
+ * constructor -- winpthreads' historical behavior: it still runs during
+ * CRT init, merely unordered relative to other constructors. */
+__attribute__((constructor))
+#else
 __attribute__((constructor(0)))
 #endif
+#endif
 static void winpthreads_init(void)
 {
     HMODULE mod = GetModuleHandleA("kernel32.dll");
